                                                          -----------   Array vs pointer    ------------


|        Ex:                  |       char s[] = "hello";                      |                  const char *p = "HELLO                        |

| Type                        |          char array                            |                     pointer array                              |
| sizeof                      |      6 bytes (5 chars + `'\0'`)                |          8 bytes on 64-bit (4 on a 32-bit MCU)                 |
| Name means                  |    base address of the array                   |       the address it holds (points to the literal)             |
| Where the chars live        |   the array's own copy, on the stack           |   a read-only literal (code/`.rodata`, i.e. flash on an MCU)   |
| s[1] = 'E' /  p[1] = 'e'    |             works                              |                 crashes (segfault)                             |
| s = "mnop" / p = "MNOP"     |         compile error                          |                         works                                  |



-->  s[i] is just *(s + i), and the same goes for p[i].

 Things I got wrong first, or found interesting

- Insert loop off-by-one.     In my first version the shift started at len - 1, so the '\0' never moved and the string had no end. It has to start at len + 1. Drawing the boxes on paper is what showed me.
- i-- after deleting.         After a shift the next character slides into position i; without i--, two digits in a row (12) leave one behind.
- scanf(" %c").               The space before %c skips the leftover newline, otherwise  %c reads '\n'.
- %19s instead of %s.         Plain  %s can overflow the buffer. Small habit, but it matters in embedded work.
- p = s; p[1] = 'e'; is fine. The crash only happens when - p points to a literal. What matters is what the pointer points at, not that it's a pointer.
- Why const char * :          the compiler catches the illegal write for me instead of the hardware doing it at runtime.

